; The open-table series' ACCEPTANCE fixture: CIP-25, spelled as the consumer's end-state spec spells
; it, with generated open tables at all FOUR payload levels. What it accepts is not any single
; emitter but the composition — an alias-of-marker custom codec keying a typed row, the
; `@custom_wire_major` declaration steering that row's dispatch, four levels of
; typed-row-plus-catch-all nesting, the delivered open struct-map rest row at the v2 wrapper and the
; details levels, and a v1/v2 type choice discriminated purely by whether the v1 arm's typed row
; accepts the payload's keys. In the consumer, all of that is ~700 lines of hand-written
; preserve-fidelity serialization behind an extern marker.
;
; Two deliberate deviations from the consumer's own spec, both narrowing rather than widening:
;
;   * the two identity types are LOCAL raw-bytes markers (tests/external_rust_raw_bytes_cip25)
;     instead of a dependency's exported types — this fixture must build standing alone, and the
;     cross-crate seam is a separate fixture's subject;
;   * the metadatum-shaped capture type is spelled here (`md`) instead of being an extern. The
;     consumer's `transaction_metadatum` STAYS extern in its end state, so this local stand-in is
;     narrower than the real one: `md`'s map arm is INLINE, because naming it as its own rule (a
;     union used as a table key) is a pre-existing generation limitation, and an inline map arm
;     inside a type choice does not carry a `@duplicates preserve` policy. The consumer's own noisy
;     pin vectors are therefore out of reach here — they carry duplicate keys inside a captured
;     metadatum map — and the acceptance oracle is the real ON-CHAIN golden instead, which is the
;     stronger vector anyway: bytes nobody on either side of this feature authored.
;
; Generated --preserve-encodings --canonical-form --wasm=false. Vectors in tests.rs.

policy_id = _CDDL_CODEGEN_RAW_BYTES_TYPE_
asset_name = _CDDL_CODEGEN_RAW_BYTES_TYPE_

; The v1 wire spells the same two identities as TEXT: a policy id as 56 lowercase hex digits, an
; asset name as its UTF-8 rendering. The alias-of-marker pair is the override ("this rule IS that
; type, written differently on the wire"), and `@custom_wire_major text` is what the typed row's
; dispatch needs: the codec owns the wire, so `cbor_types()` would answer `bytes` about a wire
; nobody writes, and every v1 key would fall through to the catch-all.

policy_id_v1 = policy_id ; @custom_serialize write_policy_id_hex @custom_deserialize read_policy_id_hex @custom_wire_major text
asset_name_v1 = asset_name ; @custom_serialize write_asset_name_utf8 @custom_deserialize read_asset_name_utf8 @custom_wire_major text

; The metadatum-shaped capture grammar every level's catch-all row is keyed and valued on. Capture
; here is TYPED, not a blind catch-all: a key or value this grammar refuses (a float, a tag, a simple
; value, a string over 64 bytes) is a parse error at every capture site, which is exactly the ledger
; refinement the consumer's own spec states.
md = { * md => md } ; @name map
   / [* md] ; @name list
   / int
   / bytes .size (0..64)
   / text .size (0..64)

string64 = text .size (0..64) ; @newtype get
chunkable = string64 ; @name single
          / [* string64] ; @name chunked

files_details = {
  name: string64,
  mediaType: string64,
  src: chunkable,
  * md => md ; @duplicates preserve
}

metadata_details = {
  name: string64,
  image: chunkable,
  ? mediaType: string64,
  ? description: chunkable,
  ? files: [* files_details],
  * md => md ; @duplicates preserve
}

; The four levels the feature exists for. Each is one typed table row plus one trailing typed
; catch-all: the typed row claims exactly its key's major (text for v1's two codec'd keys, bytes for
; v2's two raw-bytes keys) and the catch-all sees the complement, so an entry of the wrong major is
; CAPTURED while an entry of the right major that the row's type refuses is a HARD error. That second
; half is where the generated semantics deliberately diverge from the consumer's hand reader, and
; tests.rs states the divergence at every level beside its positive control.
v1_assets = {
  * asset_name_v1 => metadata_details,
  * md => md ; @duplicates preserve
}

v1 = {
  * policy_id_v1 => v1_assets,
  * md => md ; @duplicates preserve
}

v2_assets = {
  * asset_name => metadata_details,
  * md => md ; @duplicates preserve
}

v2_data = {
  * policy_id => v2_assets,
  * md => md ; @duplicates preserve
}

v2 = {
  data: v2_data,
  version: 2,
  * md => md ; @duplicates preserve
}

; The version discrimination, with no version peek anywhere in it: the choice tries v1 first, whose
; typed row claims major 3, so a v2 payload's `data`/`version` TEXT keys bind that row and the hex
; codec refuses them — a hard error, which fails the arm and rewinds. That the failure propagates
; out of an open table rather than being swallowed as capture is what makes the shape compose with a
; backtracking type choice at all.
label_metadata = v1 ; @name v1
               / v2 ; @name v2

cip25 = {
  721: label_metadata,
  * uint => md ; @duplicates preserve
}
